## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates end-to-end diagnosis and mitigation of TLS handshake tail latency in multi-tenant Linux environments under production-like load conditions.

## Key Concepts
- TLS performance optimization, session resumption, and CPU pinning
- Hardware crypto offload and the kernel crypto API
- eBPF observability with per-tenant accounting
- Canary rollouts, staged rollouts, and rollback procedures

## Code Example
```bash
# Trace TLS handshake events with BPF (conceptual)
bpftrace -e 'tracepoint:tls:handshake {
  @tenant_id = comm;
  @latency = hist(nsecs - args->start_time);
}'
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical, beginner-friendly plan for per-tenant log growth control without code changes; emphasizes safe rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Filesystem quotas
- logrotate
- tmpfiles.d cleanup
- Rollback strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple per-container log dir monitor (mock)
const {execSync} = require('child_process');
const threshold = 500*1024*1024;
const containers = ['ct1','ct2','ct3'];
containers.forEach(ct=>{
  const dir = `/var/log/${ct}`;
  try {
    const out = execSync(`du -sb ${dir}`).toString().trim();
    const size = parseInt(out.split('\t')[0],10);
    if(size>threshold) console.log(`${ct} over limit: ${size}`);
  } catch(e){}
});
```

